# Bloodborne on Debian (trixie) ARM64 rootfs

This rootfs contains the bbport runtime and nothing else you did not put there.

## What is here

- `/opt/bbport` — the runtime: `bin/bbport` is the entry point, `bin/bbport-driver`
  manages imported Vulkan drivers, `share/bbport/` holds the runtime scripts.
- `/usr/local/bin/bloodborne-launch` — prepares a data directory and starts the runtime.
- `apt` — a normal Debian package manager.

## What is not here

- No game files. You need your own Bloodborne CUSA03173 v1.09 dump.
- No Vulkan driver. This is deliberate; see below.

## Installing a Vulkan driver

Either install one from Debian:

    apt-get update
    apt-get install -y mesa-vulkan-drivers

or import a Linux/rootfs driver package:

    /opt/bbport/bin/bbport-driver import /path/to/linux-vulkan-driver.zip

`bbport-driver` rejects Android-app and AdrenoTools-only packages: those inject into
Android's graphics stack and expose no Linux Vulkan ICD. You need a package containing
ICD JSON files and aarch64 driver libraries.

## Running

    BB_GAME_DIR=/games/CUSA03173 /usr/local/bin/bloodborne-launch

Useful environment variables:

- `BB_GAME_DIR` — directory holding `eboot.bin`
- `BB_DATA_DIR` — writable directory for saves, settings, shader cache and imported
  drivers. Defaults to `/opt/bbport-data`. Keep it on real storage: FUSE-backed paths
  such as `/sdcard` make loading and shader cache access slow.
- `BB_ANDROID_ROOTFS_PROFILE=0` — disable the Android rootfs performance profile
- `BB_FRAME_STATS=1` — per-frame statistics
- `BB_BREADCRUMBS=1` — GPU command trace, for diagnosing a hang
- `BB_SAVE_LOG=1` — write this run's output to `$BB_DATA_DIR/logs/`

## Diagnostics

    /opt/bbport/bin/bbport --vulkan-info
    /opt/bbport/bin/bbport-driver list
    vulkaninfo --summary